description:
FILED s521a (Opus 5.5, 2026-10-18) from #3431's acceptance runs: IO-Compress t/105oneshot-*.t still stop at LexFile's "NO!!!!" after #3431 (oneshot.pl:85 `my $lex1 = LexFile->new( my $in );` inside a block of a sub that declared `$in` earlier).  PRE-EXISTING (both routes, also the plain-statement arm).

  sub X::n { shift; print defined $_[0] ? "D " : "U "; $_[0] = "y"; 1 }
  sub t { my $in; $in = "outer"; { my $l = X->n(my $in); print "inner=$in\n"; } print "outer=$in\n"; { X->n(my $in); print "s=$in\n" } }
  t();
perl: `U inner=y` / `outer=outer` / `U s=y`.   PCL: `D inner=y` / `outer=y` / `D s=y`.

CAUSE: Pl/Parser2.pm -- both the plain-statement embedded-`my` arm and #3431's declaration route (`_decl_init_embedded_my_names`) FILTER OUT a name already in `_let_bound_vars`, so an embedded `my` that shadows an enclosing block's lexical binds nothing and the statement writes the OUTER variable.  perl: every `my` is a new variable (in the same scope too, with a "masks earlier declaration" warning).
FIX SHAPE: drop the `_let_bound_vars` filter (keep `_file_lex_renamed` / promoted); the declaration route then needs a re-entry guard keyed on the statement (refaddr), since it re-lowers [$first, @rest].  Check the VarAnnotator verdict a shadowing decl gets (_decl_fresh reads $vi by NAME).  Bar: the probe = perl; IO-Compress 105oneshot-gzip.t / -zip.t / -deflate.t / -rawdeflate.t one-file runs past row 8; corpus-diff explained; the sweep (declaration family).

ROUND 3 (s523, 2026-10-20): the 105oneshot family still FLAPS between its two states -- in the board run deflate / gzip / zip read 1/0 (the *PLAN* state) and rawdeflate 4/4 (rows 3/5/6/7); alone on the same tree (review/r3-105oneshot.log) deflate reads 4/4 and gzip 1/0.  The blessed file now carries BOTH states for all four siblings (rawdeflate's rows added s523 by copying deflate's, its stale *FILE* row out), so a round diff shows the flap as a few NEW / FIXED / LOST rows that move between runs: not a mover.

=== s533d note (1c port 2) ===
IO-Compress t/010examples-zlib.t now runs 6 of 20 rows (was 2): the file `my ($file1, $file2, $stderr)` is no longer over-promoted (#3802), so `check`'s `new LexFile my $stderr` no longer sees the file's (defined) cell on the FIRST call.  It still croaks "NO!!!!" on a later call: the sub's embedded `my $stderr` takes the embedded-`my` veto path -- ONE global cell `p-defcell $stderr`, no per-call `let` -- so the second call's `my $stderr` is still defined.  Minimal (scratch/s533d/pr/L1.pl in the s533d worktree): `sub check { my $lex = new LexFile my $stderr; ... } check "a"; check "b";` -- perl a/b/c/d, tree dies at the 2nd call (base at the 1st).  Board row edited by s533d.
